<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Dumlupınar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-turkish-war-1919-1922">Greco-Turkish War (1919–1922)</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Dumlupınar was the decisive battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-turkish-war-1919-1922">Greco-Turkish War</a>, fought from 26 to 30 August 1922 in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. In the Great Offensive, Turkish forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal</a> broke the Greek lines near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afyonkarahisar">Afyonkarahisar</a> and routed the Greek army at Dumlupınar. The Greek retreat to the coast ended with the Turkish capture of Smyrna on 9 September, and 30 August is celebrated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> as Victory Day.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-dumlupinar.jpg" alt="Battle of Dumlupınar" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Volki16 at Turkish Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kocatepe1922.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sakarya">Battle of Sakarya</a> in August and September 1921, the Greek Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> under General Anastasios Papoulas fell back to a defensive line running from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izmit">İzmit</a> to Eskişehir and Kara Hisâr, present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afyonkarahisar">Afyonkarahisar</a>. The line formed an arc of some 700 km over hilly ground. A single-track railway ran from Kara Hisâr through Dumlupınar to Smyrna on the coast and was the main supply route of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>. Greek morale was low: many men had been under arms for years, and no quick end to the war was in sight.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal</a>, Commander-in-Chief of the government at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a>, resisted pressure to attack and used the pause to strengthen his forces and draw French and Italian sympathy towards the Turks, which isolated the pro-Greek British. For the offensive he reinforced the First Army under Nureddin Pasha against the southern flank of the Greek salient at Kara Hisâr. The Greek army under Lieutenant General Georgios Hatzianestis numbered about 220,000 men but was short of heavy artillery; the Turkish Western Front had about 208,000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On the night of 25 to 26 August the Turkish V Cavalry Corps passed through the Kirka gorge behind the Greek lines and cut the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> and the railway to Smyrna. On 26 August a heavy artillery barrage was followed by an attack of seven Turkish divisions on two Greek ones, and on 27 August the Turkish IV Corps broke through at the peak of Erkmentepe. The Greek I Corps ordered a withdrawal and Kara Hisâr was evacuated. With communications broken, the Greek headquarters at Smyrna issued orders that no longer matched the situation, and a gap opened between the groups of Major General Frangou and Major General Nikolaos Trikoupis.</p><p>The Trikoupis Group was encircled and fought all day on 29 August without breaking through. On 30 August near Alıören Turkish artillery inflicted heavy losses on its crowded columns, and the Turks counted over 2,000 Greek dead on the field the next day. Trikoupis surrendered with 5,000 to 6,000 men on 2 September; brought before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal</a>, he learned that he had been appointed commander-in-chief of the Greek army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>By 7 September the Greek army had suffered 50,000 casualties, 35,000 killed and wounded and 15,000 captured, while Turkish casualties between 26 August and 9 September were 13,476. The last Greek troops left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> from Erdek and Biga on 18 September. The Armistice of Mudanya was signed on 11 October 1922, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> was forced to accede to it on 14 October.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Dumlupınar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Greek defeat at <b>Dumlupinar</b> in August 1922, Turkish forces entered Smyrna on 9 September.</blockquote><a href="/state/smyrna-zone">Smyrna Zone</a></li></ul></section>
